From: tokenscope — https://github.com/PS12007/tokenscope
Subject: [layout control, do not merge] a 48-byte shift, reproducing F24's exact alignment

This is not a proposal. It is the second layout arm for audit issue M6, and it
closes the one caveat FINDINGS F49 left open.

F49 measured a **16-byte** shift of everything after `ggml_compute_forward_mul_mat`
and found **-0.08% [-0.16, +0.00]** on decode, identical to a matched null. But
alignment effects need not be linear in the offset, and F24's arm is not a
16-byte shift. In terms of where the hottest function in an F32 decode lands:

  build            ggml_vec_dot_f32     mod 64
  stock            0x290fc0             0
  F24's arm        0x290fb0             48
  F49's 16b arm    0x290fd0             16
  THIS 48b arm     0x290ff0             48     <- same as F24

So F49 showed that moving that function from alignment 0 to 16 costs nothing.
This tests alignment **48**, which is F24's actual case, with no behaviour
change at all.

What this does. Three never-called functions instead of one, occupying 48 bytes
between `mul_mat` and `mul_mat_id`. Unique bodies so `/OPT:ICF` cannot fold them
onto each other or onto anything else; volatile pointers so `/OPT:REF` cannot
drop them.

Verified against the linker map before measuring, which is the step F38's
control skipped and the reason it was not a control:

  - exactly two address deltas across all `.text` functions: 0 for 6,689 of
    them and **+48** for 7,730
  - the only new symbols are the three pads themselves
  - `ggml_vec_dot_f32` lands at 0x290ff0, which is **48 mod 64**, matching
    F24's arm exactly

Read it the same way as `patches/05`: measured against stock with a matched null
in the same session, `--blocks 6 -n 10`, gate first. If this is null too, layout
is not what moves F24's number at either alignment it has been tested at.

--- a/ggml/src/ggml-cpu/ggml-cpu.c
+++ b/ggml/src/ggml-cpu/ggml-cpu.c
@@ -1459,6 +1459,20 @@ UseGgmlGemm2:;
     }
 }
 
+// [tokenscope layout control -- DO NOT MERGE] Three never-called functions,
+// occupying 48 bytes so that everything after ggml_compute_forward_mul_mat
+// shifts by 48 and ggml_vec_dot_f32 lands at 48 mod 64 -- the same alignment
+// F24's patched arm gives it. FINDINGS F49 tested a 16-byte shift and found
+// nothing; this tests F24's actual alignment. Unique bodies so /OPT:ICF cannot
+// fold them, kept alive by volatile pointers so /OPT:REF cannot drop them.
+volatile int ggml_ts_layout_sink;
+static void ggml_ts_layout_pad_a(void) { ggml_ts_layout_sink = 0x5a17c0de; }
+static void ggml_ts_layout_pad_b(void) { ggml_ts_layout_sink = 0x5a17c0df; }
+static void ggml_ts_layout_pad_c(void) { ggml_ts_layout_sink = 0x5a17c0e0; }
+void (* volatile ggml_ts_layout_keep_a)(void) = ggml_ts_layout_pad_a;
+void (* volatile ggml_ts_layout_keep_b)(void) = ggml_ts_layout_pad_b;
+void (* volatile ggml_ts_layout_keep_c)(void) = ggml_ts_layout_pad_c;
+
 // ggml_compute_forward_mul_mat_id
 
 #define MMID_MATRIX_ROW(row_id, i1) matrix_rows[(row_id)*ids->ne[0]*ids->ne[1] + (i1)]
